# Test: can a female read a male's pattern?
# A test run by hand from the control page, to check the one thing you cannot see from
# outside - whether a female actually recognises the male in front of her. You choose the
# male and the female, press start, and watch her own lights, which the test borrows as a
# readout: head blue for male 1, head red for male 2, orange belly if she read "O",
# greenish belly if she read "P".
# Run it with the installation itself stopped - see the warning near the end.

0 You pick the sending male and the receiving female on the control page and press start. The page immediately shows the test as running, before anything has moved.
6 Three things move at once and the test waits for all three: the bar slides to bring that male to his meeting point with that female, and both bodies turn back to their own resting direction, so that the two end up facing each other. This takes a few seconds - longer if the bar has to cross to one of the far pairs.
0 If any of the three has not arrived within 30 seconds, the test carries on regardless and writes a warning in the log. So when a run looks wrong, read the log first: it will tell you whether the pair was ever actually in position.
0 Both of the male's appetites are forced to full, so he asks for both at once.
0 His ring lights up white and starts blinking - one step every half second, a full pattern every 5 seconds, repeating.
0 The female starts reading the light in front of her.
0 From here neither body turns again for the whole run: they stay pointed at each other while he blinks. This is worth confirming by eye at the start of every run - stand where her head is pointing and check that the blinking ring really is in that line. If a resting direction is miscalibrated the pair will be facing slightly past each other, and the test will faithfully measure nothing.
5 For the first five seconds she cannot recognise anything - she needs one whole pattern before she can even try.
1 From then on, once a second, a line is written to the results file and her readout lights are updated.
0 Her readout follows what she is seeing now: it lights up when she recognises someone, stays lit while she keeps seeing them, and goes dark again about ten seconds after she stops seeing anything. Watching it go dark is the readout working, not failing - block her view with your hand and it should go out within about ten seconds.
0 The count on the page reads like "12 correct / 3 wrong / 25 nothing seen, out of 40 seconds" - one entry for each second of the run, so the three numbers always add up to the seconds elapsed. Expect the first five seconds to be "nothing seen": that is the blind window while she fills her memory. If a long run is mostly "nothing seen", she is not seeing him - check the aiming first, before suspecting the pattern.
0 A run always starts from nothing: no recognition, no lights, an empty memory. Nothing is carried over from an earlier run.
0 The results file is only complete once you press stop - it is written out at the end, so do not expect to watch it fill up line by line while the run is going.
0 She can also confidently name the wrong male. Two of the eight patterns are the same sequence of flashes started at a different moment, and she is allowed one wrong flash in ten - so the answer she gives is not reliable even when everything else is right.
0 Warning: if the installation itself is running, this test fights it. The males start calling on their own, and the bar sets off wandering - sliding the male straight out of the alignment this test just made. Stop the installation before running this test.
0 Pressing stop ends it: the male's ring goes out, her readout lights go out, the bar stays where it is.
